const e="uil-texas-contests",c=2026,t="Read from the 2025-26 UIL Constitution and Contest Rules. Not printed: entry fees (regional sites set their own), dates for the year (the official calendar on the UIL website), venues, marks per judging criterion (in the event handbooks), ages (only grades). The constitution's athletics, music, spirit, finance and waiver sections are outside this event structure. Phone numbers and e-mail addresses are the UIL's published office lines; no private person's details are copied.",n=JSON.parse(`{"Title":"Rules & Criteria","Subtitle":"University Interscholastic League (Texas): High School academic, speech, journalism and theatre contests, and A+ contests for elementary and junior high.","NoticeTitle":"Please Read Before Registering","Notice":"Contest procedures, topics, reading lists and judging criteria are in the official contest handbooks on {{UILWebsite}}. Check the official calendar at {{OfficialCalendar}} and the entry deadline: {{EntryDeadline}}.","Notes":["Questions on academics: {{AcademicsContact}}.","Questions on theatre: {{TheatreContact}}.","Eligibility questions: {{EligibilityContact}}."],"Sections":[{"Key":"general","Title":"General Rules","Items":["UIL academic contests exist to motivate students through comprehensive competitions, challenge them to think critically and let them show mastery of essential knowledge and skills.","Participants follow the Academic Contest Ethics Code, which carries the force of rule: fairness and sportsmanship in letter and in spirit, no unethical tactics, acceptance of officials' and judges' decisions without protest, and grace in victory or defeat.","Conduct that berates, intimidates or threatens competitors has no place in interscholastic activities.","Academic district, regional and state meets take precedence over non-qualifying contests or meets.","A school must tell the regional or state meet director by the end of the second school day after the district or regional competition if a student or team knows it will not compete at the next higher meet.","A salary or stipend arrangement that gives a coach, director or sponsor a financial interest in winning a UIL contest breaks the Academic Ethics Code.","Contest procedures and rules for each event are in the official contest handbook of that event. The rules in this document and the notices in the Leaguer are official notice to all UIL members.","Invitational meets are practice meets hosted by schools. The host school controls entry, rules and administration, and should state whether UIL Set A, Set B or original materials are used. Only hosting schools may buy UIL invitational materials. Students should compete on the same set of UIL materials only once.","Schools may not sponsor students in a UIL contest, or an academic contest like one offered by the UIL, on a Sunday, unless the UIL office approves it for unavoidable hardship. Students may compete on a Sunday in college- or university-sponsored contests (and Robotics) that do not count on UIL standing, up to four such competitions a school year, with the prior approval of the superintendent or designated administrator.","Schools and individuals may not record (audio or video) speech contests or Congress sessions. The UIL may record them for educational purposes.","A school must file a report after an automated external defibrillator (AED) is used at any UIL event, practice or competition."]},{"Key":"eligibility","Title":"Who Can Enter","Items":["High school academic contests are open to students in grades 9-12 who are eligible under Subchapter M of the constitution. One-Act Play and Robotics have their own exceptions in their handbooks.","Only schools that are UIL members can enter the district meet.","A student must not be a high school graduate, must be a full-time day student of the member high school they represent (or a home-schooled student who meets Texas Education Code section 33.0832), and must be in regular attendance since the sixth class day or for 15 or more calendar days before the contest.","A student must follow State Board of Education rules on credits and grades, hold the required credits in the first six weeks of school, and be enrolled in a four-year program of high school courses.","A student must have first enrolled in ninth grade not more than four years ago, or tenth grade not more than three years ago.","A student must not have been recruited and must not be in breach of the awards rules.","A student must not have changed schools to take part in a UIL academic contest.","First six weeks of the year: a ninth grader or below must have been promoted. A second-year student needs five accumulated credits, a third-year student ten (or five earned in the last twelve months), a fourth-year student fifteen (or five earned in the last twelve months). Students who do not meet this may ask for a hardship appeal through the UIL.","A home-schooled student seeking to take part in the first six weeks must show grade-level proficiency on a nationally recognised norm-referenced test before the first day of school.","If a student's eligibility is questioned, the student has the burden to show by the preponderance of the evidence that they are eligible.","Students with disabilities served under Section 504 or the Americans with Disabilities Act may apply to the UIL staff for accommodations.","A student who graduates during the year is still eligible for UIL post-district competition if they qualified on or before the date they graduated.","A+ contests (elementary, middle and junior high) are open to students in the grade levels set for each contest, below ninth grade, who have been in attendance and are passing all classes under state law. The UIL has no age limit and no residence rule for elementary academic competition.","An elementary student may compete one grade level higher than their own grade, but not in two or more grade levels of the same contest in the same year.","Junior high students may compete at a grade level higher than the one they are enrolled in.","Junior high, middle and elementary schools are members through their parent high school. A school system with no high school pays the membership fee set in the constitution.","Combined or composite teams are not allowed in UIL district academic competitions: students represent their own campus."]},{"Key":"registration","Title":"How to Register","Items":["The designated administrator of each school submits the contestants' entries in the UIL Academic Meet Online Entry System: {{EntryDeadline}}.","See each contest plan for the number of students a school may enter and any special entry rules. For each contest all participants must compete at the same time and contest site.","Substitution: an eligible student whose name is not on the online entry form may substitute with a Substitute Eligibility Form (from the UIL website) or a letter signed by the designated administrator certifying eligibility, given to the contest director before the student competes.","A student already certified on the entry forms who moves to another event needs written notice signed by the event sponsor or a school representative.","A late entry (late online entry form, late entry of an event, or late entry of an individual to fill a vacant place) needs approval from the district academic meet director. Allowances must be the same for every school in the district. The coach brings a letter signed by the superintendent or designated administrator certifying eligibility and the approval.","Regional and state entries: the district (or regional) meet director's online results are the school's official entry. A separate school entry is not needed.","If a qualified first, second or third place contestant cannot go to the next meet, the designated administrator tells the district (or state) director in writing. The next lower place finisher then takes the place. Alternates who are not taking the place of an absent qualifier may not compete.","Each regional site has its own fee structure and payment instructions. The rulebook does not print a fee amount.","A+ districts: schools tell the UIL office they wish to take part by registering before the date on the official calendar ({{AplusRegistrationDeadline}}). A district may design its own entry form or use the form in the A+ Handbook, and sets its own due dates, late-entry and substitution policies.","Congress: a school files an intent to participate form on the UIL Congress webpage by the posted date. Entries are submitted at least ten calendar days before the congressional region competition through the UIL website."]},{"Key":"levels","Title":"Rounds and Qualification","Items":["High school academic competition runs district meet, regional meet and state meet. Academic district assignments follow the academic alignments on the UIL website.","Winners in each conference or division qualify to the next higher meet. Consult the handbooks for Theatrical Design, Film, Congress, Robotics and Barbara Jordan Historical Essay.","Individuals who advance: two Cross-Examination Debate teams (district to state); three each of Lincoln-Douglas Debate, Extemporaneous Informative Speaking, Extemporaneous Persuasive Speaking, Poetry Interpretation, Prose Interpretation, Editorial, Feature, Headline and News Writing, Copy Editing, Accounting, Calculator Applications, Computer Science, Current Issues and Events, Literary Criticism, Mathematics, Number Sense, Ready Writing, Social Studies and Spelling and Vocabulary.","Three One-Act Plays advance from district (two plays from region to state).","Science: three overall winners advance, plus the top scorer in each of biology, chemistry and physics.","Teams: in events with a team component (not Cross-Examination Debate, One-Act Play, Robotics or Theatrical Design) the first-place team and a wild-card team advance. The wild card is the highest-scoring second-place team from each region (to regional) or each conference (to state).","The four highest-ranking members of the winning team advance. A first place or wild-card team may advance only as many members as took part in the qualifying meet.","Team members may compete for individual honours at each level whether they advanced as team members or individuals, except in journalism and speech.","If a first-place team cannot go to the next meet, the alternate team is told and allowed to compete. Only one substitution may be made for team members who cannot compete at the next level, in all team events except One-Act Play, with a signed Substitute Eligibility Form or letter.","A contestant disqualified for breaking a contest rule does not advance, is not replaced by a substitute and earns no points.","Results must be submitted and certified as final in the UIL Academic Meet Online Entry System. Contestants whose results are not submitted do not advance. {{ResultsDeadline}} for district results.","Official results are announced at the end of the verification period and cannot be changed afterwards. The verification period checks for scoring, tabulation and data-entry errors; it is not a chance to challenge a test or answer key. A student or coach who is absent forfeits the right to verify.","Mistakes on an answer key of an objectively scored contest are corrected and papers judged on correctness.","A+ contests: elementary and junior high contestants do not qualify to a higher post-district meet. The winner of a junior high One-Act Play contest does not advance beyond district.","District organisation dates: the district chair and academic meet director are named to the UIL office by {{DistrictChairDeadline}}; meet sites and dates are chosen by {{MeetSiteDatesDeadline}}; the Cross-Examination Debate meet is set up by {{CxMeetSetupDeadline}} and the academic meet by {{AcademicMeetSetupDeadline}}. Dates are on {{OfficialCalendar}}."]},{"Key":"teams","Title":"Team Rules","Items":["A school must have at least three contestants compete in a contest to take part in its team competition. The four highest-ranking members of the winning team advance.","Team ties are broken through third place: the highest overall net or objective score of the fourth-ranking member breaks the tie. A team that does not have a fourth member cannot take part in the tiebreaker. If fourth members have the same score, the tie stands and all those involved advance.","A+ team competition (optional, chosen by the district): the combined score of the three contestants in a division from a school is the team score. Art and Music Memory teams use the combined score of all contestants divided by the number of contestants, with at least three competing.","Cross-Examination Team Debate teams have two members; at state, a substituted debater must pair with a member of the original district team."]},{"Key":"points","Title":"Points, Ties and Championships","Items":["High school points are awarded to schools for first to sixth place. Individual contests: first 15, second 12, third 10, fourth 8, fifth 6, sixth 4 (Barbara Jordan Essay 10, 8, 6, 4, 2, 1). Cross-Examination Debate 20, 16, 12, 10, 8, 6 (no points at regional). Team contests score 10 for first and 5 for second; Computer Science team 20, 16, 12; Robotics team 20, 16, 12, 10.","Science: overall 15, 12, 10, 8, 6, 4; top scorer in biology, chemistry and physics 3 points each; team 10 and 5.","Speech sweepstakes: in the combined speech events at each meet, 10 points go to the school with the most overall points and 5 points to the second. Journalism: 10 and 5 in the same way. Theatre sweepstakes (state meet only): 10 and 5. The third school is recognised but gets no points.","Congress: points are awarded at the state meet only (15, 12, 10, 8, 6, 4; top presiding officer 10). Theatrical Design (individual 15, 12, 10, 8, 6, 4; group 20, 16, 12, 10, 8, 6) and Film (20, 16, 12, 10, 8, 6) points are awarded at the state meet only.","One-Act Play points: each of the three unranked advancing plays from district (two from region) receives 20 points and the alternate play 15. At state, plays are ranked through sixth place: 20, 16, 12, 10, 8, 6. Individual acting awards: each best performer 10, each all-star cast 8, each honorable mention all-star cast 6. These points are not awarded for zone, bi-district or area contests.","Ties: schools of contestants tied for first add first and second place points and divide equally; the next contestant receives third place points; ties for other places are handled in the same way, and a tie for the last paying place divides that place's points equally.","Ties are broken through sixth place for individuals in contests that have tiebreaker procedures (see each contest). After applying tiebreakers, a tie for first means there is no second place; a tie for second means no third; a tie for third means no fourth and both third-place winners advance.","The academic championship goes to the school with the most points at that meet. A school must place in more than one event to be eligible for the regional or state academic championship. The state championship is awarded by conference.","A+ points are awarded for every place a contestant took part in: first 15, second 12, third 10, fourth 8, fifth 6, sixth 4 for individuals; team 8, 6, 4 for first to third. Ties split the points of the places concerned. No school is eligible for the overall A+ championship unless it places in at least three events. Points won by an elementary, middle or junior high school do not count towards a high school meet, and the other way round.","A+ One-Act Play points: ranked plays 22, 18, 15 for first to third; individual awards 8, 4, 2 (best performer, all-star cast, honorable mention all-star cast)."]},{"Key":"prizes","Title":"Awards","Items":["A regional academic champion trophy goes to the school with the highest regional points, and a state academic championship trophy goes to each conference champion.","At state, the first-place entries in each of the five journalism contests in conferences 1A to 6A are judged a second time, and the top entry receives the championship plaque known as Tops in Texas.","Barbara Jordan Historical Essay state finalists receive state awards and become eligible to apply for Texas Interscholastic League Foundation scholarships.","Theatrical Design: a plaque and individual medals go to the top six groups; medals go to first to sixth place individual winners.","High school students may receive a certificate, medal, trophy, ring or other symbolic award from the school, district executive committee, the organiser of the competition, the UIL or an approved outside source. Awards schools may give: {{SchoolAwardLimits}}.","A+: students in grades 2 to 6 have no awards rule; the schools decide. Junior high students may not receive awards in excess of $10 per year (total for all UIL school activities) from the school, but may accept symbolic awards such as ribbons, plaques and medals from the organisation conducting the contest.","Students may accept unlimited awards for invitational academic or fine arts contests that do not count on UIL standing and for intraschool competitions."]},{"Key":"conduct","Title":"Conduct and Contest Materials","Items":["Coaches, contest directors and contestants must keep contest materials confidential. Passing on information about the tests breaks the Academic Ethics Code and is penalised. A contestant who removes the test entry from the contest area before judging is disqualified.","In objective contests, contestants not present when tests are distributed are disqualified in Calculator Applications and Number Sense.","Journalism contests: each contestant gets a contest number, the only identification allowed on the paper. Writing the student's name or school name on an entry is disqualification; leaving off the number may be too. Judges do not see the roster until judging is complete.","Written contests with computers (Ready Writing, News, Feature and Editorial Writing): contestants bring their own portable printer, hardware, software and paper. Spell check, thesaurus and dictionary functions may be used. The print command must be started by the time contest time expires; no cables or commands may be changed after time ends. A computer failure means the rest of the time can be used by hand or on another computer.","Handwritten entries use the contestant's own 8 1/2 x 11 inch ruled or typing paper (or paper from the host), in ink, on one side only.","Debate is public. Competing debaters may not watch rounds of the tournament in which they are not debating. Only the judge and the debaters take notes, except in the final debates. Debaters and judges may not give or accept notes taken during the tournament. Scouting is grounds for disqualification of the team.","No coaching during a debate. Prompting means a loss in the round. Time signals are not prompting.","In extemporaneous speaking and Congress, laptops and electronic retrieval devices may be used only with wired and wireless connections disabled. Cell phones, smartphones and smartwatches are not allowed in the preparation room or the chamber.","Questions about a contest go to the contest director before the judges' decision is announced. The decision of the meet officials is final and no arguments with judges are permitted. Excessive abuse by contestants or coaches can lead to disqualification of the school and suspension for the following year.","A+ contests: a district may add or delete UIL contests from its plan. Contest directors may not depart from the Constitution and Contest Rules. Critiques should first comment on the positive aspects and then offer constructive criticism.","A+ answer key: if graders, judges and coaches agree an answer on the key is wrong, the contest director may correct it before the official results are announced. Students scoring 100% in Art, Music Memory or Spelling at district may receive a Certificate of Achievement."]},{"Key":"complaints","Title":"Protests and Appeals","Items":["The district executive committee decides protests and reports of violations in the district, including the Sunday participation rule. Disputes between schools of different districts are settled with equal representation from each district; the schools involved do not vote.","A protest must be filed in writing by the superintendent or designated administrator, within a reasonable time not more than four days after the contest unless it involves eligibility or a rule with mandatory penalties (junior high rules).","Penalties for breaking the rules are those in Sections 27 and 29 of the constitution. The minimum penalty for using an ineligible contestant is forfeiture of the contest (junior high).","The decision of the critic judge or judges in One-Act Play is final."]},{"Key":"other","Title":"Other UIL Programs (not in this template)","Items":["Music contests (marching band, solo and ensemble, concert organisation and sight-reading, state music festivals) are rated in the music handbook and are not part of this event structure.","High school and junior high athletics, High School Spirit and Robotics are separate programs with their own handbooks.","The Young Filmmakers Festival (Film) takes three entries per school in each category defined in the Young Filmmakers Guide; its categories, deadlines and entry procedure are published on the UIL website.","High school conferences are set by enrolment: {{ConferenceSizes}}. Schools compete against others in their own conference.","The Interscholastic League Press Conference (ILPC) is a voluntary state scholastic publications association; schools do not need to join it to take part in UIL journalism contests."]}],"Documents":[{"Title":"UIL Constitution and Contest Rules 2025-26","Url":"https://resources.finalsite.net/images/v1758137358/kcisdnet/qeobiutrjudepwfq0o3y/2025-2026UILConstitutionandContestRules.pdf"},{"Title":"UIL official calendar","Url":"https://www.uiltexas.org/calendar"}]}`),o=[{ParticipantType:"Individual",Source:"UIL Constitution and Contest Rules 2025-26, Sections 902-905, 1408",Sections:{About:null,TimeAndFormat:null,Rules:["Each member high school may enter as many as six individuals in most academic contests at the district meet (four individuals in Number Sense, or six with district executive committee approval; three in Ready Writing, speech and journalism contests).","A+ contests: a school may enter three contestants per division, or five in Art and Music Memory, in the district meet.","A student may enter at most two speech events and only one event in each speech category: debate, interpretation and extemporaneous speaking. Congress is not counted."],Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:null,Points:null,Notes:null},Markdown:`## Rules
- Each member high school may enter as many as six individuals in most academic contests at the district meet (four individuals in Number Sense, or six with district executive committee approval; three in Ready Writing, speech and journalism contests).
- A+ contests: a school may enter three contestants per division, or five in Art and Music Memory, in the district meet.
- A student may enter at most two speech events and only one event in each speech category: debate, interpretation and extemporaneous speaking. Congress is not counted.`},{ParticipantType:"Team",Source:"UIL Constitution and Contest Rules 2025-26, Section 902(h)(3), 1408",Sections:{About:null,TimeAndFormat:null,Rules:["A team needs at least three contestants from a school competing in the contest. The four highest-ranking members of the winning team advance.","A+ team competition is held only if the district chooses it."],Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:null,Points:null,Notes:null},Markdown:`## Rules
- A team needs at least three contestants from a school competing in the contest. The four highest-ranking members of the winning team advance.
- A+ team competition is held only if the district chooses it.`}],i=[{AgeGroup:"Elementary Gr 2-5 Individual",Source:"UIL Constitution and Contest Rules 2025-26, Section 1401(d)",Sections:{About:null,TimeAndFormat:null,Rules:["Elementary contests cover grades 2 to 5 here (grade 2 Creative Writing, Storytelling and Music Memory, grade 3 Ready Writing and Spelling, grade 4 Art, Number Sense and Oral Reading, grade 5 Dictionary Skills, Listening, Maps Graphs and Charts and Social Studies). Ages are not printed; the template ages are placeholders.","Districts offer either a separate division for each grade level or combined grade-level divisions."],Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:null,Points:null,Notes:null},Markdown:`## Rules
- Elementary contests cover grades 2 to 5 here (grade 2 Creative Writing, Storytelling and Music Memory, grade 3 Ready Writing and Spelling, grade 4 Art, Number Sense and Oral Reading, grade 5 Dictionary Skills, Listening, Maps Graphs and Charts and Social Studies). Ages are not printed; the template ages are placeholders.
- Districts offer either a separate division for each grade level or combined grade-level divisions.`},{AgeGroup:"Elementary Gr 2-5 Team",Source:"UIL Constitution and Contest Rules 2025-26, Section 1408",Sections:{About:null,TimeAndFormat:null,Rules:["Elementary team competition is optional and chosen by the district."],Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:null,Points:null,Notes:null},Markdown:`## Rules
- Elementary team competition is optional and chosen by the district.`},{AgeGroup:"Junior High Gr 6-8 Individual",Source:"UIL Constitution and Contest Rules 2025-26, Section 1400, 1401(d)",Sections:{About:null,TimeAndFormat:null,Rules:["Junior high contests cover grades 6 to 8. Students must have been in attendance and be passing the courses required by state law. Home-schooled students may take part if they follow Texas Education Code section 33.0832.","Districts offer either a separate division for each grade level or combined grade-level divisions.","A district may use no more than one school day or two half-school days a year for district academic contests, and no contest may start before the end of the academic school day for other competitions.","Junior high schools are not assigned to districts or conferences by the UIL office. They may follow the high school academic districts or form their own."],Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:null,Points:null,Notes:null},Markdown:`## Rules
- Junior high contests cover grades 6 to 8. Students must have been in attendance and be passing the courses required by state law. Home-schooled students may take part if they follow Texas Education Code section 33.0832.
- Districts offer either a separate division for each grade level or combined grade-level divisions.
- A district may use no more than one school day or two half-school days a year for district academic contests, and no contest may start before the end of the academic school day for other competitions.
- Junior high schools are not assigned to districts or conferences by the UIL office. They may follow the high school academic districts or form their own.`},{AgeGroup:"Junior High Gr 6-8 Team",Source:"UIL Constitution and Contest Rules 2025-26, Section 1408",Sections:{About:null,TimeAndFormat:null,Rules:["Junior high team competition is optional and chosen by the district."],Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:null,Points:null,Notes:null},Markdown:`## Rules
- Junior high team competition is optional and chosen by the district.`},{AgeGroup:"High School Gr 9-12 Individual",Source:"UIL Constitution and Contest Rules 2025-26, Sections 902, 1000",Sections:{About:null,TimeAndFormat:null,Rules:["High school contests are for grades 9-12. Students in speech may enter two events with the limits listed on this page."],Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:null,Points:null,Notes:null},Markdown:`## Rules
- High school contests are for grades 9-12. Students in speech may enter two events with the limits listed on this page.`},{AgeGroup:"High School Gr 9-12 Team",Source:"UIL Constitution and Contest Rules 2025-26, Section 902(h)(3)",Sections:{About:null,TimeAndFormat:null,Rules:["Team contests need at least three contestants from the school."],Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:null,Points:null,Notes:null},Markdown:`## Rules
- Team contests need at least three contestants from the school.`}],s=JSON.parse(`[{"Title":"Accounting","Source":"UIL Constitution and Contest Rules 2025-26, Section 920","Sections":{"About":["Principles and practices of accounting for sole proprietorships, partnerships and corporations. May include bookkeeping terminology, the worksheet with adjustments, income statement, balance sheet, trial balance, account classification, journalizing, posting, bank reconciliation, payroll and other basic accounting cycle items."],"TimeAndFormat":null,"Rules":["Calculator restrictions are in the contest handbook.","Up to six individuals per school at district (four with district executive committee approval)."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":["No individual ties are broken."],"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- Principles and practices of accounting for sole proprietorships, partnerships and corporations. May include bookkeeping terminology, the worksheet with adjustments, income statement, balance sheet, trial balance, account classification, journalizing, posting, bank reconciliation, payroll and other basic accounting cycle items.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- Calculator restrictions are in the contest handbook.\\n- Up to six individuals per school at district (four with district executive committee approval).\\n\\n## Judging\\n- No individual ties are broken."},{"Title":"Barbara Jordan Historical Essay","Source":"UIL Constitution and Contest Rules 2025-26, Section 965","Sections":{"About":["Research paper on the contributions of an individual, organisation or movement that made significant and positive contributions to Texas history or culture. Students should look first to their local communities for topics."],"TimeAndFormat":null,"Rules":["Research paper format is required; guidelines and minimum standards are on the UIL website. Essays should show original research; primary sources and interviews are strongly encouraged.","Any student in grades 9-12 at a UIL member high school may enter.","Entries are submitted electronically on or before the deadline published on the UIL website."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":["All entries that meet basic requirements are judged and judges return comments. Judges nominate essays and a selection committee names the state finalists."],"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- Research paper on the contributions of an individual, organisation or movement that made significant and positive contributions to Texas history or culture. Students should look first to their local communities for topics.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- Research paper format is required; guidelines and minimum standards are on the UIL website. Essays should show original research; primary sources and interviews are strongly encouraged.\\n- Any student in grades 9-12 at a UIL member high school may enter.\\n- Entries are submitted electronically on or before the deadline published on the UIL website.\\n\\n## Judging\\n- All entries that meet basic requirements are judged and judges return comments. Judges nominate essays and a selection committee names the state finalists."},{"Title":"Calculator Applications","Source":"UIL Constitution and Contest Rules 2025-26, Section 924","Sections":{"About":["Efficient problem solving with calculations in engineering, science and mathematics: arithmetic, roots, powers, logarithms, trigonometric functions, iterative solutions, calculus, elementary statistics and matrix algebra, plus geometric and stated problems."],"TimeAndFormat":null,"Rules":["Calculator restrictions are in the contest handbook.","Qualified contestants not present when the tests are distributed are disqualified.","Up to six individuals per school at district (four with approval)."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":["Individual ties: the higher place goes to the contestant with the most points on stated and geometric problems."],"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- Efficient problem solving with calculations in engineering, science and mathematics: arithmetic, roots, powers, logarithms, trigonometric functions, iterative solutions, calculus, elementary statistics and matrix algebra, plus geometric and stated problems.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- Calculator restrictions are in the contest handbook.\\n- Qualified contestants not present when the tests are distributed are disqualified.\\n- Up to six individuals per school at district (four with approval).\\n\\n## Judging\\n- Individual ties: the higher place goes to the contestant with the most points on stated and geometric problems."},{"Title":"Computer Science","Source":"UIL Constitution and Contest Rules 2025-26, Section 928","Sections":{"About":["A written exam for individuals and teams plus a team programming session on computer science programming knowledge and skills."],"TimeAndFormat":null,"Rules":["Teams bring their own media for submitting solutions and a computer with all hardware, cables and software. Each team uses one computer. A printer is allowed but not required.","All team members take the written exam. With four members, the coach chooses which three take part in programming. Schools that do not take part in the programming session cannot place as a team, unless only one school entered enough contestants for a team.","Calculators are not permitted; the computer's own calculation functions may be used while programming."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":["Individual ties use percent accuracy (problems correct divided by problems attempted). Team ties use the programming scores first, then the written exam scores, then the total written exam score."],"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- A written exam for individuals and teams plus a team programming session on computer science programming knowledge and skills.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- Teams bring their own media for submitting solutions and a computer with all hardware, cables and software. Each team uses one computer. A printer is allowed but not required.\\n- All team members take the written exam. With four members, the coach chooses which three take part in programming. Schools that do not take part in the programming session cannot place as a team, unless only one school entered enough contestants for a team.\\n- Calculators are not permitted; the computer's own calculation functions may be used while programming.\\n\\n## Judging\\n- Individual ties use percent accuracy (problems correct divided by problems attempted). Team ties use the programming scores first, then the written exam scores, then the total written exam score."},{"Title":"Congress","Source":"UIL Constitution and Contest Rules 2025-26, Section 1008","Sections":{"About":["Contestants formulate legislative bills and resolutions, debate them and vote, in a chamber modelled on the United States Congress with parliamentary procedure."],"TimeAndFormat":null,"Rules":["Each school may enter five competitors in the congressional region competition and register two alternates. If ten or fewer contestants compete in a conference in a region, both alternates from schools with a full entry may be seated; with 11-15 entries only one alternate.","Congress is organised by the twenty Educational Service Center regions. Each conference in a region can advance at least three representatives to state.","Entering Congress does not affect eligibility for any other UIL high school academic event.","Congress is public; no coaching or cheering is allowed. Laptops and tablets are allowed with connections disabled; phones and smartwatches are not."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":null,"Points":["Congress points are awarded at the state meet only."],"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- Contestants formulate legislative bills and resolutions, debate them and vote, in a chamber modelled on the United States Congress with parliamentary procedure.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- Each school may enter five competitors in the congressional region competition and register two alternates. If ten or fewer contestants compete in a conference in a region, both alternates from schools with a full entry may be seated; with 11-15 entries only one alternate.\\n- Congress is organised by the twenty Educational Service Center regions. Each conference in a region can advance at least three representatives to state.\\n- Entering Congress does not affect eligibility for any other UIL high school academic event.\\n- Congress is public; no coaching or cheering is allowed. Laptops and tablets are allowed with connections disabled; phones and smartwatches are not.\\n\\n## Points\\n- Congress points are awarded at the state meet only."},{"Title":"Current Issues and Events","Source":"UIL Constitution and Contest Rules 2025-26, Section 932","Sections":{"About":["Objective questions and an essay on important domestic and international issues, events and personalities, from news sites, newspapers, periodicals and journals."],"TimeAndFormat":null,"Rules":["A contestant who does not hand in an essay is disqualified.","Judges read the essays of the eight contestants with the highest objective scores (more if there is a tie for eighth). The essay score is added to the objective score."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":["Ties are broken through sixth place; tied essays are judged one against the other."],"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- Objective questions and an essay on important domestic and international issues, events and personalities, from news sites, newspapers, periodicals and journals.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- A contestant who does not hand in an essay is disqualified.\\n- Judges read the essays of the eight contestants with the highest objective scores (more if there is a tie for eighth). The essay score is added to the objective score.\\n\\n## Judging\\n- Ties are broken through sixth place; tied essays are judged one against the other."},{"Title":"Literary Criticism","Source":"UIL Constitution and Contest Rules 2025-26, Section 940","Sections":{"About":["Objective questions and an essay on literary history and critical terms, from the reading list announced each year on the UIL website, plus analysis of passages from works not on the list."],"TimeAndFormat":null,"Rules":["A contestant who does not hand in an essay is disqualified. No points are awarded for the essay."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":["The essay is judged only for contestants tied on the objective score; three judges who do not coach the tied contestants rank the essays. Ties are broken through sixth place."],"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- Objective questions and an essay on literary history and critical terms, from the reading list announced each year on the UIL website, plus analysis of passages from works not on the list.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- A contestant who does not hand in an essay is disqualified. No points are awarded for the essay.\\n\\n## Judging\\n- The essay is judged only for contestants tied on the objective score; three judges who do not coach the tied contestants rank the essays. Ties are broken through sixth place."},{"Title":"Mathematics","Source":"UIL Constitution and Contest Rules 2025-26, Section 942","Sections":{"About":["Objective questions on Algebra I, Geometry, Algebra II, Pre-calculus, AP Calculus (BC), AP Statistics and director's choice topics."],"TimeAndFormat":null,"Rules":["Calculator restrictions are in the contest handbook."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":["Ties use percent accuracy (correct divided by attempted)."],"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- Objective questions on Algebra I, Geometry, Algebra II, Pre-calculus, AP Calculus (BC), AP Statistics and director's choice topics.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- Calculator restrictions are in the contest handbook.\\n\\n## Judging\\n- Ties use percent accuracy (correct divided by attempted)."},{"Title":"Number Sense","Source":"UIL Constitution and Contest Rules 2025-26, Section 944","Sections":{"About":["Mental calculation with no calculators, computers, or pen and paper, covering basic mathematics, algebra, geometry, trigonometry, analysis, number theory and calculus."],"TimeAndFormat":null,"Rules":["Qualified contestants not present when tests are distributed are disqualified.","Up to four individuals per school at district, or six with district executive committee approval."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":["No individual ties are broken."],"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- Mental calculation with no calculators, computers, or pen and paper, covering basic mathematics, algebra, geometry, trigonometry, analysis, number theory and calculus.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- Qualified contestants not present when tests are distributed are disqualified.\\n- Up to four individuals per school at district, or six with district executive committee approval.\\n\\n## Judging\\n- No individual ties are broken."},{"Title":"Ready Writing","Source":"UIL Constitution and Contest Rules 2025-26, Section 948","Sections":{"About":["Contestants choose between two prompts (excerpts from literature, publications or speeches) and write an expository composition that explains, proves or explores the topic in a balanced way."],"TimeAndFormat":null,"Rules":["Up to three individuals per school at district. There is no team competition.","Printed thesaurus and dictionary, or the spell check, thesaurus and dictionary of a laptop, may be used. No other resources.","Computers are not provided at state; contestants bring their own laptops and portable printers."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":["Judges rank papers through sixth place. There can be no ties."],"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- Contestants choose between two prompts (excerpts from literature, publications or speeches) and write an expository composition that explains, proves or explores the topic in a balanced way.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- Up to three individuals per school at district. There is no team competition.\\n- Printed thesaurus and dictionary, or the spell check, thesaurus and dictionary of a laptop, may be used. No other resources.\\n- Computers are not provided at state; contestants bring their own laptops and portable printers.\\n\\n## Judging\\n- Judges rank papers through sixth place. There can be no ties."},{"Title":"Science","Source":"UIL Constitution and Contest Rules 2025-26, Section 952","Sections":{"About":["Objective questions on fundamental principles of biology, chemistry and physics."],"TimeAndFormat":null,"Rules":["Papers may be turned in 30 minutes after the contest starts. Calculator restrictions are in the contest handbook.","Up to six individuals per school at district.","The top scorer in each of biology, chemistry and physics qualifies and competes equally with the overall winners; one alternate is named for each."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":["Ties use percent accuracy; top-scorer ties use percent accuracy within the subject."],"Points":["Top scorer in biology, chemistry and physics: 3 points each."],"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- Objective questions on fundamental principles of biology, chemistry and physics.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- Papers may be turned in 30 minutes after the contest starts. Calculator restrictions are in the contest handbook.\\n- Up to six individuals per school at district.\\n- The top scorer in each of biology, chemistry and physics qualifies and competes equally with the overall winners; one alternate is named for each.\\n\\n## Judging\\n- Ties use percent accuracy; top-scorer ties use percent accuracy within the subject.\\n\\n## Points\\n- Top scorer in biology, chemistry and physics: 3 points each."},{"Title":"Social Studies","Source":"UIL Constitution and Contest Rules 2025-26, Section 956","Sections":{"About":["Objective questions and an essay on history, geography, civics and economics, based on a theme announced each year on the UIL website."],"TimeAndFormat":null,"Rules":["A contestant who does not hand in an essay is disqualified.","Judges read the essays of the eight contestants with the highest objective scores (more if there is a tie for eighth). The essay score is added to the objective score."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":["Ties are broken through sixth place; tied essays are judged one against the other."],"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- Objective questions and an essay on history, geography, civics and economics, based on a theme announced each year on the UIL website.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- A contestant who does not hand in an essay is disqualified.\\n- Judges read the essays of the eight contestants with the highest objective scores (more if there is a tie for eighth). The essay score is added to the objective score.\\n\\n## Judging\\n- Ties are broken through sixth place; tied essays are judged one against the other."},{"Title":"Spelling and Vocabulary","Source":"UIL Constitution and Contest Rules 2025-26, Section 960","Sections":{"About":["Objective proofreading and vocabulary questions and words written from dictation."],"TimeAndFormat":null,"Rules":["At least 80 percent of the test comes from UIL Word Power. Other words may include common words, words in the news and words that build vocabulary."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":["A tie for first to sixth is broken by the score on Part III. If a tie remains, it stands."],"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- Objective proofreading and vocabulary questions and words written from dictation.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- At least 80 percent of the test comes from UIL Word Power. Other words may include common words, words in the news and words that build vocabulary.\\n\\n## Judging\\n- A tie for first to sixth is broken by the score on Part III. If a tie remains, it stands."},{"Title":"Cross-Examination Team Debate","Source":"UIL Constitution and Contest Rules 2025-26, Sections 1000-1001","Sections":{"About":["Two-member teams debate a policy resolution posted on the UIL website. Round robin or several preliminary rounds lead to an elimination bracket. Each round is about 90 minutes of oral arguments; each team argues both the affirmative and the negative."],"TimeAndFormat":null,"Rules":["In each conference a school may enter three two-member teams at district; in districts with fewer than eight teams, a school with a full entry may enter a fourth team.","Team debaters may not enter Lincoln-Douglas debate.","No substitutions are allowed once a tournament has begun. At state, the remaining debater must be a member of the original district team.","A coach who does not tell the district contest director in time that a team will not compete may cause disqualification and suspension from team debate the next year.","Two teams qualify from district to state. No Cross-Examination Debate points are awarded at regional meets."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":null,"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- Two-member teams debate a policy resolution posted on the UIL website. Round robin or several preliminary rounds lead to an elimination bracket. Each round is about 90 minutes of oral arguments; each team argues both the affirmative and the negative.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- In each conference a school may enter three two-member teams at district; in districts with fewer than eight teams, a school with a full entry may enter a fourth team.\\n- Team debaters may not enter Lincoln-Douglas debate.\\n- No substitutions are allowed once a tournament has begun. At state, the remaining debater must be a member of the original district team.\\n- A coach who does not tell the district contest director in time that a team will not compete may cause disqualification and suspension from team debate the next year.\\n- Two teams qualify from district to state. No Cross-Examination Debate points are awarded at regional meets."},{"Title":"Lincoln-Douglas Debate","Source":"UIL Constitution and Contest Rules 2025-26, Sections 1000, 1002","Sections":{"About":["One-on-one value debate. Each round is about 40 minutes; each debater argues both sides of the resolution during the tournament. Two topics, one for fall and one for spring, are posted on the UIL website."],"TimeAndFormat":null,"Rules":["A school may enter three individuals at district; in districts with fewer than eight debaters, a school with a full entry may enter a fourth.","A Lincoln-Douglas debater may not also enter Cross-Examination Team Debate, Prose or Poetry Interpretation.","No substitutions once a tournament has begun and none after the district meet."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":null,"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- One-on-one value debate. Each round is about 40 minutes; each debater argues both sides of the resolution during the tournament. Two topics, one for fall and one for spring, are posted on the UIL website.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- A school may enter three individuals at district; in districts with fewer than eight debaters, a school with a full entry may enter a fourth.\\n- A Lincoln-Douglas debater may not also enter Cross-Examination Team Debate, Prose or Poetry Interpretation.\\n- No substitutions once a tournament has begun and none after the district meet."},{"Title":"Extemporaneous Informative Speaking","Source":"UIL Constitution and Contest Rules 2025-26, Sections 1003-1005","Sections":{"About":["Contestants draw five topics on current state, national and international events, choose one and have thirty minutes to prepare a speech of at most seven minutes."],"TimeAndFormat":null,"Rules":["Each school may enter three students. Only high school students may enter. A contestant may not compete in more than one extemporaneous event in the same year at district.","One 3x5 inch note card is allowed; using more than one card is disqualification.","The speaker may finish only the sentence in progress after time ends. There is no minimum time.","Informative speakers present the facts impartially; persuasive speakers argue a point of view."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":null,"Points":null,"Notes":["Preliminary rounds are held when nine or more students enter: sections of no more than eight; 9-16 entries 2 sections (top three of each advance); 15-24 entries 3 sections (top two); 20-32 entries 4 sections (top two); 32-39 entries 8 sections (winner of each)."]},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- Contestants draw five topics on current state, national and international events, choose one and have thirty minutes to prepare a speech of at most seven minutes.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- Each school may enter three students. Only high school students may enter. A contestant may not compete in more than one extemporaneous event in the same year at district.\\n- One 3x5 inch note card is allowed; using more than one card is disqualification.\\n- The speaker may finish only the sentence in progress after time ends. There is no minimum time.\\n- Informative speakers present the facts impartially; persuasive speakers argue a point of view.\\n\\n## Notes for participants\\n- Preliminary rounds are held when nine or more students enter: sections of no more than eight; 9-16 entries 2 sections (top three of each advance); 15-24 entries 3 sections (top two); 20-32 entries 4 sections (top two); 32-39 entries 8 sections (winner of each)."},{"Title":"Extemporaneous Persuasive Speaking","Source":"UIL Constitution and Contest Rules 2025-26, Sections 1003-1005","Sections":{"About":["Contestants draw five topics on current state, national and international events, choose one and have thirty minutes to prepare a speech of at most seven minutes."],"TimeAndFormat":null,"Rules":["Each school may enter three students. Only high school students may enter. A contestant may not compete in more than one extemporaneous event in the same year at district.","One 3x5 inch note card is allowed; using more than one card is disqualification.","The speaker may finish only the sentence in progress after time ends. There is no minimum time.","Informative speakers present the facts impartially; persuasive speakers argue a point of view."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":null,"Points":null,"Notes":["Preliminary rounds are held when nine or more students enter: sections of no more than eight; 9-16 entries 2 sections (top three of each advance); 15-24 entries 3 sections (top two); 20-32 entries 4 sections (top two); 32-39 entries 8 sections (winner of each)."]},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- Contestants draw five topics on current state, national and international events, choose one and have thirty minutes to prepare a speech of at most seven minutes.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- Each school may enter three students. Only high school students may enter. A contestant may not compete in more than one extemporaneous event in the same year at district.\\n- One 3x5 inch note card is allowed; using more than one card is disqualification.\\n- The speaker may finish only the sentence in progress after time ends. There is no minimum time.\\n- Informative speakers present the facts impartially; persuasive speakers argue a point of view.\\n\\n## Notes for participants\\n- Preliminary rounds are held when nine or more students enter: sections of no more than eight; 9-16 entries 2 sections (top three of each advance); 15-24 entries 3 sections (top two); 20-32 entries 4 sections (top two); 32-39 entries 8 sections (winner of each)."},{"Title":"Poetry Interpretation","Source":"UIL Constitution and Contest Rules 2025-26, Sections 1003, 1006-1007","Sections":{"About":["Oral interpretation of literature from two categories (A and B) that the contestant must prepare. In each round the contestant reads from the category selected for that round."],"TimeAndFormat":null,"Rules":["Each school may enter three students. A contestant who reads outside the selected category is disqualified.","An introduction naming the writer and selection is required in both categories.","Contestants read from manuscripts or copies in a binder, not from books or magazines.","The performance including introduction and transitions is at most seven minutes with no grace period. Exceeding it is disqualification from the round, except in the state final where the contestant is placed last.","Students may not use selections from the same literary work more than one year at the state meet.","Poetry contestants may not compete in Prose Interpretation or Lincoln-Douglas debate at district, and the other way round."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":null,"Points":null,"Notes":["Preliminary and final round sections follow the same chart as the other individual speech contests."]},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- Oral interpretation of literature from two categories (A and B) that the contestant must prepare. In each round the contestant reads from the category selected for that round.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- Each school may enter three students. A contestant who reads outside the selected category is disqualified.\\n- An introduction naming the writer and selection is required in both categories.\\n- Contestants read from manuscripts or copies in a binder, not from books or magazines.\\n- The performance including introduction and transitions is at most seven minutes with no grace period. Exceeding it is disqualification from the round, except in the state final where the contestant is placed last.\\n- Students may not use selections from the same literary work more than one year at the state meet.\\n- Poetry contestants may not compete in Prose Interpretation or Lincoln-Douglas debate at district, and the other way round.\\n\\n## Notes for participants\\n- Preliminary and final round sections follow the same chart as the other individual speech contests."},{"Title":"Prose Interpretation","Source":"UIL Constitution and Contest Rules 2025-26, Sections 1003, 1006-1007","Sections":{"About":["Oral interpretation of literature from two categories (A and B) that the contestant must prepare. In each round the contestant reads from the category selected for that round."],"TimeAndFormat":null,"Rules":["Each school may enter three students. A contestant who reads outside the selected category is disqualified.","An introduction naming the writer and selection is required in both categories.","Contestants read from manuscripts or copies in a binder, not from books or magazines.","The performance including introduction and transitions is at most seven minutes with no grace period. Exceeding it is disqualification from the round, except in the state final where the contestant is placed last.","Students may not use selections from the same literary work more than one year at the state meet.","Poetry contestants may not compete in Prose Interpretation or Lincoln-Douglas debate at district, and the other way round."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":null,"Points":null,"Notes":["Preliminary and final round sections follow the same chart as the other individual speech contests."]},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- Oral interpretation of literature from two categories (A and B) that the contestant must prepare. In each round the contestant reads from the category selected for that round.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- Each school may enter three students. A contestant who reads outside the selected category is disqualified.\\n- An introduction naming the writer and selection is required in both categories.\\n- Contestants read from manuscripts or copies in a binder, not from books or magazines.\\n- The performance including introduction and transitions is at most seven minutes with no grace period. Exceeding it is disqualification from the round, except in the state final where the contestant is placed last.\\n- Students may not use selections from the same literary work more than one year at the state meet.\\n- Poetry contestants may not compete in Prose Interpretation or Lincoln-Douglas debate at district, and the other way round.\\n\\n## Notes for participants\\n- Preliminary and final round sections follow the same chart as the other individual speech contests."},{"Title":"Editorial Writing","Source":"UIL Constitution and Contest Rules 2025-26, Sections 1023-1028","Sections":{"About":["Journalism contests: News Writing, Feature Writing and Editorial Writing give a fact sheet from which a story is written; Headline Writing gives six short articles and prescribed headlines; Copy Editing has objective questions, proofreading sentences and editing a news brief."],"TimeAndFormat":null,"Rules":["Each school may enter three persons in each of the five journalism contests at district. A student may compete in all five.","Contestants may use a thesaurus, dictionary and Associated Press Stylebook (electronic or printed in News, Feature and Editorial; printed only in Headline and Copy Editing). No electronic device in Headline or Copy Editing.","Judges read and critique all papers and rank the top six; there can be no ties. A judging panel of three is recommended; at regional and state, one judge must be a current or former high school journalism teacher."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":null,"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- Journalism contests: News Writing, Feature Writing and Editorial Writing give a fact sheet from which a story is written; Headline Writing gives six short articles and prescribed headlines; Copy Editing has objective questions, proofreading sentences and editing a news brief.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- Each school may enter three persons in each of the five journalism contests at district. A student may compete in all five.\\n- Contestants may use a thesaurus, dictionary and Associated Press Stylebook (electronic or printed in News, Feature and Editorial; printed only in Headline and Copy Editing). No electronic device in Headline or Copy Editing.\\n- Judges read and critique all papers and rank the top six; there can be no ties. A judging panel of three is recommended; at regional and state, one judge must be a current or former high school journalism teacher."},{"Title":"Feature Writing","Source":"UIL Constitution and Contest Rules 2025-26, Sections 1023-1028","Sections":{"About":["Journalism contests: News Writing, Feature Writing and Editorial Writing give a fact sheet from which a story is written; Headline Writing gives six short articles and prescribed headlines; Copy Editing has objective questions, proofreading sentences and editing a news brief."],"TimeAndFormat":null,"Rules":["Each school may enter three persons in each of the five journalism contests at district. A student may compete in all five.","Contestants may use a thesaurus, dictionary and Associated Press Stylebook (electronic or printed in News, Feature and Editorial; printed only in Headline and Copy Editing). No electronic device in Headline or Copy Editing.","Judges read and critique all papers and rank the top six; there can be no ties. A judging panel of three is recommended; at regional and state, one judge must be a current or former high school journalism teacher."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":null,"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- Journalism contests: News Writing, Feature Writing and Editorial Writing give a fact sheet from which a story is written; Headline Writing gives six short articles and prescribed headlines; Copy Editing has objective questions, proofreading sentences and editing a news brief.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- Each school may enter three persons in each of the five journalism contests at district. A student may compete in all five.\\n- Contestants may use a thesaurus, dictionary and Associated Press Stylebook (electronic or printed in News, Feature and Editorial; printed only in Headline and Copy Editing). No electronic device in Headline or Copy Editing.\\n- Judges read and critique all papers and rank the top six; there can be no ties. A judging panel of three is recommended; at regional and state, one judge must be a current or former high school journalism teacher."},{"Title":"Headline Writing","Source":"UIL Constitution and Contest Rules 2025-26, Sections 1023-1028","Sections":{"About":["Journalism contests: News Writing, Feature Writing and Editorial Writing give a fact sheet from which a story is written; Headline Writing gives six short articles and prescribed headlines; Copy Editing has objective questions, proofreading sentences and editing a news brief."],"TimeAndFormat":null,"Rules":["Each school may enter three persons in each of the five journalism contests at district. A student may compete in all five.","Contestants may use a thesaurus, dictionary and Associated Press Stylebook (electronic or printed in News, Feature and Editorial; printed only in Headline and Copy Editing). No electronic device in Headline or Copy Editing.","Judges read and critique all papers and rank the top six; there can be no ties. A judging panel of three is recommended; at regional and state, one judge must be a current or former high school journalism teacher."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":null,"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- Journalism contests: News Writing, Feature Writing and Editorial Writing give a fact sheet from which a story is written; Headline Writing gives six short articles and prescribed headlines; Copy Editing has objective questions, proofreading sentences and editing a news brief.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- Each school may enter three persons in each of the five journalism contests at district. A student may compete in all five.\\n- Contestants may use a thesaurus, dictionary and Associated Press Stylebook (electronic or printed in News, Feature and Editorial; printed only in Headline and Copy Editing). No electronic device in Headline or Copy Editing.\\n- Judges read and critique all papers and rank the top six; there can be no ties. A judging panel of three is recommended; at regional and state, one judge must be a current or former high school journalism teacher."},{"Title":"News Writing","Source":"UIL Constitution and Contest Rules 2025-26, Sections 1023-1028","Sections":{"About":["Journalism contests: News Writing, Feature Writing and Editorial Writing give a fact sheet from which a story is written; Headline Writing gives six short articles and prescribed headlines; Copy Editing has objective questions, proofreading sentences and editing a news brief."],"TimeAndFormat":null,"Rules":["Each school may enter three persons in each of the five journalism contests at district. A student may compete in all five.","Contestants may use a thesaurus, dictionary and Associated Press Stylebook (electronic or printed in News, Feature and Editorial; printed only in Headline and Copy Editing). No electronic device in Headline or Copy Editing.","Judges read and critique all papers and rank the top six; there can be no ties. A judging panel of three is recommended; at regional and state, one judge must be a current or former high school journalism teacher."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":null,"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- Journalism contests: News Writing, Feature Writing and Editorial Writing give a fact sheet from which a story is written; Headline Writing gives six short articles and prescribed headlines; Copy Editing has objective questions, proofreading sentences and editing a news brief.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- Each school may enter three persons in each of the five journalism contests at district. A student may compete in all five.\\n- Contestants may use a thesaurus, dictionary and Associated Press Stylebook (electronic or printed in News, Feature and Editorial; printed only in Headline and Copy Editing). No electronic device in Headline or Copy Editing.\\n- Judges read and critique all papers and rank the top six; there can be no ties. A judging panel of three is recommended; at regional and state, one judge must be a current or former high school journalism teacher."},{"Title":"Copy Editing","Source":"UIL Constitution and Contest Rules 2025-26, Sections 1023-1028","Sections":{"About":["Journalism contests: News Writing, Feature Writing and Editorial Writing give a fact sheet from which a story is written; Headline Writing gives six short articles and prescribed headlines; Copy Editing has objective questions, proofreading sentences and editing a news brief."],"TimeAndFormat":null,"Rules":["Each school may enter three persons in each of the five journalism contests at district. A student may compete in all five.","Contestants may use a thesaurus, dictionary and Associated Press Stylebook (electronic or printed in News, Feature and Editorial; printed only in Headline and Copy Editing). No electronic device in Headline or Copy Editing.","Judges read and critique all papers and rank the top six; there can be no ties. A judging panel of three is recommended; at regional and state, one judge must be a current or former high school journalism teacher."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":null,"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- Journalism contests: News Writing, Feature Writing and Editorial Writing give a fact sheet from which a story is written; Headline Writing gives six short articles and prescribed headlines; Copy Editing has objective questions, proofreading sentences and editing a news brief.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- Each school may enter three persons in each of the five journalism contests at district. A student may compete in all five.\\n- Contestants may use a thesaurus, dictionary and Associated Press Stylebook (electronic or printed in News, Feature and Editorial; printed only in Headline and Copy Editing). No electronic device in Headline or Copy Editing.\\n- Judges read and critique all papers and rank the top six; there can be no ties. A judging panel of three is recommended; at regional and state, one judge must be a current or former high school journalism teacher."},{"Title":"One-Act Play","Source":"UIL Constitution and Contest Rules 2025-26, Sections 1033-1034","Sections":{"About":["Each school performs a short play. Plays are adjudicated by an accredited critic judge, advancing plays are announced and individual awards are given. Schools receive an evaluation form and an oral critique."],"TimeAndFormat":null,"Rules":["Each member high school may enter a play company in its conference at district. Participation means performing an approved script with a running time of at least eighteen minutes. A school that enrolls and does not take part may be suspended the next year.","Directors must be full-time employees of the school district (limited exceptions for retired teachers with 20 or more years, student teachers and full-time substitutes).","No play may exceed 40 minutes of performance, 7 minutes to set and 7 minutes to strike, and no more than 60 seconds between the set time and the beginning of performance.","Plays come from the approved lists of long and short plays; others need permission under the One-Act Play Handbook.","Real or starter firearms, explosives and combustibles may not be used; toy, wooden or model firearms may be. Directors may not be in the light booth, backstage or offstage during the performance.","Three unranked advancing plays and an alternate are chosen at zone, district, bi-district and area; two and an alternate at region. State plays are ranked through sixth place.","Individual awards: two best performers, an all-star cast and an honorable mention all-star cast. Judging is by an odd number of judges or a single critic judge from the UIL accredited list; the critic's decision is final."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":null,"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- Each school performs a short play. Plays are adjudicated by an accredited critic judge, advancing plays are announced and individual awards are given. Schools receive an evaluation form and an oral critique.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- Each member high school may enter a play company in its conference at district. Participation means performing an approved script with a running time of at least eighteen minutes. A school that enrolls and does not take part may be suspended the next year.\\n- Directors must be full-time employees of the school district (limited exceptions for retired teachers with 20 or more years, student teachers and full-time substitutes).\\n- No play may exceed 40 minutes of performance, 7 minutes to set and 7 minutes to strike, and no more than 60 seconds between the set time and the beginning of performance.\\n- Plays come from the approved lists of long and short plays; others need permission under the One-Act Play Handbook.\\n- Real or starter firearms, explosives and combustibles may not be used; toy, wooden or model firearms may be. Directors may not be in the light booth, backstage or offstage during the performance.\\n- Three unranked advancing plays and an alternate are chosen at zone, district, bi-district and area; two and an alternate at region. State plays are ranked through sixth place.\\n- Individual awards: two best performers, an all-star cast and an honorable mention all-star cast. Judging is by an odd number of judges or a single critic judge from the UIL accredited list; the critic's decision is final."},{"Title":"Theatrical Design","Source":"UIL Constitution and Contest Rules 2025-26, Section 1035","Sections":{"About":["Schools submit designs to the UIL office following the Theatrical Design Guide. Designs answer a directorial prompt for a stated title, genre or playwright, are rated by theatre and marketing professionals, and entries rated Exemplary advance to the state contest."],"TimeAndFormat":null,"Rules":["Each school may enter two individual entries in each design area and one group entry.","Sponsors must be full-time school district employees; they may guide but not produce any of the submitted material.","State entries are ranked first to sixth.","Entry deadlines are in the official calendar and the guide."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":null,"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- Schools submit designs to the UIL office following the Theatrical Design Guide. Designs answer a directorial prompt for a stated title, genre or playwright, are rated by theatre and marketing professionals, and entries rated Exemplary advance to the state contest.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- Each school may enter two individual entries in each design area and one group entry.\\n- Sponsors must be full-time school district employees; they may guide but not produce any of the submitted material.\\n- State entries are ranked first to sixth.\\n- Entry deadlines are in the official calendar and the guide."},{"Title":"Art (A+)","Source":"UIL Constitution and Contest Rules 2025-26, Section 1410","Sections":{"About":["Study of paintings from the National Gallery of Art and selected Texas museums. Part A: identify the artist and title of 15 paintings chosen at random from the official list of 30. Part B: 30 questions on art elements, principles and history."],"TimeAndFormat":["About 30 minutes for Part A and 30 minutes for Part B."],"Rules":["Up to five contestants per division per school.","Students who score 100% in both sections at district may get a Certificate of Achievement."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":["Contestants and coaches have up to 15 minutes in the verification period to compare papers with the official key, then the director announces official results, which are final."],"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- Study of paintings from the National Gallery of Art and selected Texas museums. Part A: identify the artist and title of 15 paintings chosen at random from the official list of 30. Part B: 30 questions on art elements, principles and history.\\n\\n## Time and format\\n- About 30 minutes for Part A and 30 minutes for Part B.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- Up to five contestants per division per school.\\n- Students who score 100% in both sections at district may get a Certificate of Achievement.\\n\\n## Judging\\n- Contestants and coaches have up to 15 minutes in the verification period to compare papers with the official key, then the director announces official results, which are final."},{"Title":"Calculator Applications (A+)","Source":"UIL Constitution and Contest Rules 2025-26, Section 1414","Sections":{"About":["An 80-problem test on calculations with addition, subtraction, multiplication, division, roots and powers, plus simple geometric and stated problems. Grades 6-8."],"TimeAndFormat":null,"Rules":["Up to three contestants per division per school."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":["Contestants and coaches have up to 15 minutes in the verification period to compare papers with the official key, then the director announces official results, which are final."],"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- An 80-problem test on calculations with addition, subtraction, multiplication, division, roots and powers, plus simple geometric and stated problems. Grades 6-8.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- Up to three contestants per division per school.\\n\\n## Judging\\n- Contestants and coaches have up to 15 minutes in the verification period to compare papers with the official key, then the director announces official results, which are final."},{"Title":"Chess Puzzle (A+)","Source":"UIL Constitution and Contest Rules 2025-26, Section 1416","Sections":{"About":["An objective test of about 20 chess puzzles plus a tiebreaker section."],"TimeAndFormat":["Thirty minutes plus five minutes for the tiebreaker."],"Rules":["Grades 2-8. Up to three contestants per division per school."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":["Contestants and coaches have up to 15 minutes in the verification period to compare papers with the official key, then the director announces official results, which are final."],"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- An objective test of about 20 chess puzzles plus a tiebreaker section.\\n\\n## Time and format\\n- Thirty minutes plus five minutes for the tiebreaker.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- Grades 2-8. Up to three contestants per division per school.\\n\\n## Judging\\n- Contestants and coaches have up to 15 minutes in the verification period to compare papers with the official key, then the director announces official results, which are final."},{"Title":"Creative Writing (A+)","Source":"UIL Constitution and Contest Rules 2025-26, Section 1418","Sections":{"About":["Grade 2 only. Contestants write an original story from a prompt of several captioned pictures, using at least one of the pictured items."],"TimeAndFormat":["Thirty minutes."],"Rules":["One division; up to three contestants per school. There is no team competition."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":null,"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- Grade 2 only. Contestants write an original story from a prompt of several captioned pictures, using at least one of the pictured items.\\n\\n## Time and format\\n- Thirty minutes.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- One division; up to three contestants per school. There is no team competition."},{"Title":"Dictionary Skills (A+)","Source":"UIL Constitution and Contest Rules 2025-26, Section 1422","Sections":{"About":["A 40-question objective test taken from Merriam-Webster's Intermediate Dictionary; contestants may use dictionaries in the contest."],"TimeAndFormat":["Twenty minutes."],"Rules":["Grades 5-8. Up to three contestants per division per school."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":["Contestants and coaches have up to 15 minutes in the verification period to compare papers with the official key, then the director announces official results, which are final."],"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- A 40-question objective test taken from Merriam-Webster's Intermediate Dictionary; contestants may use dictionaries in the contest.\\n\\n## Time and format\\n- Twenty minutes.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- Grades 5-8. Up to three contestants per division per school.\\n\\n## Judging\\n- Contestants and coaches have up to 15 minutes in the verification period to compare papers with the official key, then the director announces official results, which are final."},{"Title":"Editorial Writing (A+)","Source":"UIL Constitution and Contest Rules 2025-26, Section 1424","Sections":{"About":["Contestants write an editorial from a fact sheet."],"TimeAndFormat":["Forty-five minutes."],"Rules":["Grades 6-8. Up to three contestants per division per school. There is no team competition.","Contestants may handwrite or use their own computers (with printers). Electronic or printed dictionaries and thesauruses are allowed.","A single judge or an odd number of judges who are not coaches of the contestants."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":["There can be no ties."],"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- Contestants write an editorial from a fact sheet.\\n\\n## Time and format\\n- Forty-five minutes.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- Grades 6-8. Up to three contestants per division per school. There is no team competition.\\n- Contestants may handwrite or use their own computers (with printers). Electronic or printed dictionaries and thesauruses are allowed.\\n- A single judge or an odd number of judges who are not coaches of the contestants.\\n\\n## Judging\\n- There can be no ties."},{"Title":"Impromptu Speaking (A+)","Source":"UIL Constitution and Contest Rules 2025-26, Section 1426","Sections":{"About":["Contestants draw three topics, select one and have three minutes to prepare a speech given without notes."],"TimeAndFormat":["Up to five minutes per speech, no minimum. Going over is penalised one rank."],"Rules":["Grades 6-8. Up to three contestants per division per school. No costumes or props. A student may enter at most two speech events.","No coaching or prompting during the speech. Audiences are permitted. Sections of no more than eight."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":["There can be no ties."],"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- Contestants draw three topics, select one and have three minutes to prepare a speech given without notes.\\n\\n## Time and format\\n- Up to five minutes per speech, no minimum. Going over is penalised one rank.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- Grades 6-8. Up to three contestants per division per school. No costumes or props. A student may enter at most two speech events.\\n- No coaching or prompting during the speech. Audiences are permitted. Sections of no more than eight.\\n\\n## Judging\\n- There can be no ties."},{"Title":"Listening (A+)","Source":"UIL Constitution and Contest Rules 2025-26, Section 1430","Sections":{"About":["Contestants listen to a script of about seven to ten minutes, take notes and answer 25 objective questions on main idea, details, conclusions and fact versus opinion."],"TimeAndFormat":["Ten minutes for the testing period."],"Rules":["Grades 5-8. Up to three contestants per division per school."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":["Contestants and coaches have up to 15 minutes in the verification period to compare papers with the official key, then the director announces official results, which are final."],"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- Contestants listen to a script of about seven to ten minutes, take notes and answer 25 objective questions on main idea, details, conclusions and fact versus opinion.\\n\\n## Time and format\\n- Ten minutes for the testing period.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- Grades 5-8. Up to three contestants per division per school.\\n\\n## Judging\\n- Contestants and coaches have up to 15 minutes in the verification period to compare papers with the official key, then the director announces official results, which are final."},{"Title":"Maps, Graphs and Charts (A+)","Source":"UIL Constitution and Contest Rules 2025-26, Section 1434","Sections":{"About":["About 75 objective questions on world maps, pie charts, bar charts and local area maps, using an atlas."],"TimeAndFormat":["Forty-five minutes."],"Rules":["Grades 5-8. Up to three contestants per division per school. Contestants may use an atlas."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":["Contestants and coaches have up to 15 minutes in the verification period to compare papers with the official key, then the director announces official results, which are final."],"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- About 75 objective questions on world maps, pie charts, bar charts and local area maps, using an atlas.\\n\\n## Time and format\\n- Forty-five minutes.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- Grades 5-8. Up to three contestants per division per school. Contestants may use an atlas.\\n\\n## Judging\\n- Contestants and coaches have up to 15 minutes in the verification period to compare papers with the official key, then the director announces official results, which are final."},{"Title":"Mathematics (A+)","Source":"UIL Constitution and Contest Rules 2025-26, Section 1438","Sections":{"About":["Fifty multiple-choice problems on numeration, arithmetic, fractions, decimals, exponents, probability, statistics, number theory, simple interest, measurements and conversions; geometry and algebra as appropriate for the grade."],"TimeAndFormat":["Thirty minutes."],"Rules":["Grades 6-8. Up to three contestants per division per school."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":["Contestants and coaches have up to 15 minutes in the verification period to compare papers with the official key, then the director announces official results, which are final."],"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- Fifty multiple-choice problems on numeration, arithmetic, fractions, decimals, exponents, probability, statistics, number theory, simple interest, measurements and conversions; geometry and algebra as appropriate for the grade.\\n\\n## Time and format\\n- Thirty minutes.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- Grades 6-8. Up to three contestants per division per school.\\n\\n## Judging\\n- Contestants and coaches have up to 15 minutes in the verification period to compare papers with the official key, then the director announces official results, which are final."},{"Title":"Modern Oratory (A+)","Source":"UIL Constitution and Contest Rules 2025-26, Section 1442","Sections":{"About":["Contestants choose a topic from the list the UIL provides each year, weigh pro and con points and defend one side in a three to six minute speech without notes."],"TimeAndFormat":["Three to six minutes. Going over or under is penalised one rank."],"Rules":["Grades 6-8. Up to three contestants per division per school. No costumes or props. At most two speech events per student.","No coaching or prompting during the speech."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":["There can be no ties. Evaluation sheets may be returned to contestants."],"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- Contestants choose a topic from the list the UIL provides each year, weigh pro and con points and defend one side in a three to six minute speech without notes.\\n\\n## Time and format\\n- Three to six minutes. Going over or under is penalised one rank.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- Grades 6-8. Up to three contestants per division per school. No costumes or props. At most two speech events per student.\\n- No coaching or prompting during the speech.\\n\\n## Judging\\n- There can be no ties. Evaluation sheets may be returned to contestants."},{"Title":"Music Memory (A+)","Source":"UIL Constitution and Contest Rules 2025-26, Section 1446","Sections":{"About":["Listening contest on the official UIL Music Memory List. Grade 2: identify short excerpts of 10 selections. Grades 3 and 4: a matching section and up to 20 excerpts. Grades 5-8: up to 20 excerpts; formats may differ by grade."],"TimeAndFormat":null,"Rules":["Grades 2-8. Up to five contestants per division per school.","Students with a 100% correct paper on the district test selections may get a Certificate of Achievement (the four tiebreaking questions do not count)."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":["Ties are broken with the tiebreaker rules for the grade level; if a tie remains it stands."],"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- Listening contest on the official UIL Music Memory List. Grade 2: identify short excerpts of 10 selections. Grades 3 and 4: a matching section and up to 20 excerpts. Grades 5-8: up to 20 excerpts; formats may differ by grade.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- Grades 2-8. Up to five contestants per division per school.\\n- Students with a 100% correct paper on the district test selections may get a Certificate of Achievement (the four tiebreaking questions do not count).\\n\\n## Judging\\n- Ties are broken with the tiebreaker rules for the grade level; if a tie remains it stands."},{"Title":"Number Sense (A+)","Source":"UIL Constitution and Contest Rules 2025-26, Section 1450","Sections":{"About":["A fill-in-the-blank test completed mentally, with no calculation on paper or calculator. Erasures, mark-overs and mark-outs are not permitted."],"TimeAndFormat":["Exactly ten minutes."],"Rules":["Grades 4-8. Up to three contestants per division per school."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":["Contestants and coaches have up to 15 minutes in the verification period to compare papers with the official key, then the director announces official results, which are final."],"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- A fill-in-the-blank test completed mentally, with no calculation on paper or calculator. Erasures, mark-overs and mark-outs are not permitted.\\n\\n## Time and format\\n- Exactly ten minutes.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- Grades 4-8. Up to three contestants per division per school.\\n\\n## Judging\\n- Contestants and coaches have up to 15 minutes in the verification period to compare papers with the official key, then the director announces official results, which are final."},{"Title":"One-Act Play (A+)","Source":"UIL Constitution and Contest Rules 2025-26, Section 1454","Sections":{"About":["A 40-minute play produced under the One-Act Play Handbook and Sections 1033-1034, with deadlines set by the district executive committee."],"TimeAndFormat":null,"Rules":["One division. Seventh and eighth graders may enter; sixth graders may take part if on the same campus as the seventh and eighth grade.","Enrolment and Title Entry Forms are not required for junior high schools. Directors for eighth grade and below need not be full-time school district employees.","The winner does not advance beyond the district level. Plays are ranked first, second and third."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":["The decision of the critic judge or judges is final."],"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- A 40-minute play produced under the One-Act Play Handbook and Sections 1033-1034, with deadlines set by the district executive committee.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- One division. Seventh and eighth graders may enter; sixth graders may take part if on the same campus as the seventh and eighth grade.\\n- Enrolment and Title Entry Forms are not required for junior high schools. Directors for eighth grade and below need not be full-time school district employees.\\n- The winner does not advance beyond the district level. Plays are ranked first, second and third.\\n\\n## Judging\\n- The decision of the critic judge or judges is final."},{"Title":"Oral Reading (A+)","Source":"UIL Constitution and Contest Rules 2025-26, Section 1458","Sections":{"About":["Oral reading of poetry, short stories and children's fiction, with selection requirements in the official contest procedures."],"TimeAndFormat":["Up to six minutes, no minimum. Going over is penalised one rank."],"Rules":["Grades 4-8, and eligible ninth graders. Up to three contestants per division per school. No costumes or props. Students in grades four to eight may enter at most two speech events.","Sections of no more than eight; winners of preliminary rounds meet in a final round."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":["There can be no ties."],"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- Oral reading of poetry, short stories and children's fiction, with selection requirements in the official contest procedures.\\n\\n## Time and format\\n- Up to six minutes, no minimum. Going over is penalised one rank.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- Grades 4-8, and eligible ninth graders. Up to three contestants per division per school. No costumes or props. Students in grades four to eight may enter at most two speech events.\\n- Sections of no more than eight; winners of preliminary rounds meet in a final round.\\n\\n## Judging\\n- There can be no ties."},{"Title":"Ready Writing (A+)","Source":"UIL Constitution and Contest Rules 2025-26, Section 1462","Sections":{"About":["Contestants choose between two prompts that define the audience and purpose (a letter, a newspaper article, an essay for the principal) and write a composition. No minimum or maximum words."],"TimeAndFormat":["Two hours; the district executive committee may shorten it for the grade level."],"Rules":["Grades 3-8. Up to three contestants per division per school. There is no team competition.","Handwriting or own computers with printers; electronic or printed dictionaries and thesauruses are allowed."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":["There can be no ties."],"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- Contestants choose between two prompts that define the audience and purpose (a letter, a newspaper article, an essay for the principal) and write a composition. No minimum or maximum words.\\n\\n## Time and format\\n- Two hours; the district executive committee may shorten it for the grade level.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- Grades 3-8. Up to three contestants per division per school. There is no team competition.\\n- Handwriting or own computers with printers; electronic or printed dictionaries and thesauruses are allowed.\\n\\n## Judging\\n- There can be no ties."},{"Title":"Science (A+)","Source":"UIL Constitution and Contest Rules 2025-26, Section 1466","Sections":{"About":["About 50 objective questions from current state-adopted science textbooks and the curriculum."],"TimeAndFormat":["Forty-five minutes."],"Rules":["Grades 6-8. Up to three contestants per division per school."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":["Contestants and coaches have up to 15 minutes in the verification period to compare papers with the official key, then the director announces official results, which are final."],"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- About 50 objective questions from current state-adopted science textbooks and the curriculum.\\n\\n## Time and format\\n- Forty-five minutes.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- Grades 6-8. Up to three contestants per division per school.\\n\\n## Judging\\n- Contestants and coaches have up to 15 minutes in the verification period to compare papers with the official key, then the director announces official results, which are final."},{"Title":"Social Studies (A+)","Source":"UIL Constitution and Contest Rules 2025-26, Section 1468","Sections":{"About":["About 40 objective questions on history, government systems, economics, citizenship and culture from adopted textbooks and identified primary sources."],"TimeAndFormat":["Thirty minutes."],"Rules":["Grades 5-8. Up to three contestants per division per school."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":["Contestants and coaches have up to 15 minutes in the verification period to compare papers with the official key, then the director announces official results, which are final."],"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- About 40 objective questions on history, government systems, economics, citizenship and culture from adopted textbooks and identified primary sources.\\n\\n## Time and format\\n- Thirty minutes.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- Grades 5-8. Up to three contestants per division per school.\\n\\n## Judging\\n- Contestants and coaches have up to 15 minutes in the verification period to compare papers with the official key, then the director announces official results, which are final."},{"Title":"Spelling (A+)","Source":"UIL Constitution and Contest Rules 2025-26, Section 1470","Sections":{"About":["Contestants write words given by a pronouncer at about five words a minute. Grades 3-4: 50 words and a 15-word tiebreaker; grades 5-6: 80 words and 20; grades 7-8: 110 words and 30. About 20 percent of the words come from outside the UIL spelling lists."],"TimeAndFormat":null,"Rules":["Grades 3-8. Up to three contestants per division per school.","The tiebreaker is given to everyone right after the test; a contestant who does not take it is disqualified.","Students who score 100% at district may get a Certificate of Achievement."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":["Individual ties are broken with the tiebreaker of only the tied contestants."],"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- Contestants write words given by a pronouncer at about five words a minute. Grades 3-4: 50 words and a 15-word tiebreaker; grades 5-6: 80 words and 20; grades 7-8: 110 words and 30. About 20 percent of the words come from outside the UIL spelling lists.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- Grades 3-8. Up to three contestants per division per school.\\n- The tiebreaker is given to everyone right after the test; a contestant who does not take it is disqualified.\\n- Students who score 100% at district may get a Certificate of Achievement.\\n\\n## Judging\\n- Individual ties are broken with the tiebreaker of only the tied contestants."},{"Title":"Storytelling (A+)","Source":"UIL Constitution and Contest Rules 2025-26, Section 1474","Sections":{"About":["Grades 2-3. Contestants hear a story of 600 to 1,100 words read once and retell it in their own words before a judge or judges."],"TimeAndFormat":null,"Rules":["Up to three contestants per division per school. No costumes, props or notes. There is no time limit. There is no team competition.","Sections of no more than eight; winners of preliminary rounds meet in a final round."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":["There can be no ties."],"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- Grades 2-3. Contestants hear a story of 600 to 1,100 words read once and retell it in their own words before a judge or judges.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- Up to three contestants per division per school. No costumes, props or notes. There is no time limit. There is no team competition.\\n- Sections of no more than eight; winners of preliminary rounds meet in a final round.\\n\\n## Judging\\n- There can be no ties."}]`),a=[],r=[{Key:"UILOffice",Kind:"address",Label:"UIL office",Value:{Name:"University Interscholastic League",Address:"1701 Manor Road",City:"Austin, TX 78722",MapUrl:"",Directions:"Office hours 8:00 a.m. to 4:30 p.m. daily except weekends and UT-Austin holidays."},Source:"UIL Constitution and Contest Rules 2025-26, Administration (p.12)"},{Key:"AcademicsContact",Kind:"contact",Label:"Contact: academics questions",Value:{Name:"UIL Academics",Role:"Academic contest rules and eligibility (High School and A+)",Phone:"512-471-5883",WhatsApp:"",Email:"academics@uiltexas.org"},Source:"UIL Constitution and Contest Rules 2025-26, Appendix II and inside cover"},{Key:"TheatreContact",Kind:"contact",Label:"Contact: theatre questions",Value:{Name:"UIL Theatre",Role:"One-Act Play, Theatrical Design and Film",Phone:"512-471-9996",WhatsApp:"",Email:"theatre@uiltexas.org"},Source:"UIL Constitution and Contest Rules 2025-26, Appendix II and inside cover"},{Key:"EligibilityContact",Kind:"contact",Label:"Contact: administration and eligibility",Value:{Name:"UIL Administration",Role:"Eligibility and general questions",Phone:"512-471-5883",WhatsApp:"",Email:"info@uiltexas.org"},Source:"UIL Constitution and Contest Rules 2025-26, inside cover"},{Key:"OfficialCalendar",Kind:"link",Label:"UIL official calendar",Value:"https://www.uiltexas.org/calendar",Source:"UIL Constitution and Contest Rules 2025-26, Official Calendar page"},{Key:"UILWebsite",Kind:"link",Label:"UIL website (contest handbooks, topics, reading lists, online entry)",Value:"https://www.uiltexas.org",Source:"UIL Constitution and Contest Rules 2025-26, inside cover"},{Key:"DistrictChairDeadline",Kind:"date",Label:"Last date to tell the UIL office the district chair and academic meet director",Value:"October 1",Source:"UIL Constitution and Contest Rules 2025-26, Section 903(d)(2)"},{Key:"MeetSiteDatesDeadline",Kind:"date",Label:"Last date for the district committee to choose meet sites and dates",Value:"November 1",Source:"UIL Constitution and Contest Rules 2025-26, Section 903(d)(3)"},{Key:"CxMeetSetupDeadline",Kind:"date",Label:"Last date for the district Cross-Examination Debate director to set up the meet",Value:"December 1",Source:"UIL Constitution and Contest Rules 2025-26, Section 903(e)(1)"},{Key:"AcademicMeetSetupDeadline",Kind:"date",Label:"Last date for the district academic meet director to set up the academic meet online",Value:"February 1",Source:"UIL Constitution and Contest Rules 2025-26, Section 903(e)(1)"},{Key:"EntryDeadline",Kind:"text",Label:"Entry deadline for district academic events",Value:"no later than 10 calendar days before the day of the meet, through the UIL Academic Meet Online Entry System",Source:"UIL Constitution and Contest Rules 2025-26, Section 903(f)(1)"},{Key:"ResultsDeadline",Kind:"text",Label:"When district results must be certified",Value:"online by 5 p.m. of the day following the academic meet",Source:"UIL Constitution and Contest Rules 2025-26, Section 903(e)(1)"},{Key:"AplusRegistrationDeadline",Kind:"text",Label:"A+ registration deadline",Value:"the date on the official UIL calendar",Source:"UIL Constitution and Contest Rules 2025-26, Section 1401(e)(2)"},{Key:"AplusMaterialWindows",Kind:"text",Label:"A+ district contest material windows",Value:"fall/winter district meets held December 1 to January 31; spring district meets held February 1 to the Saturday before Memorial Day",Source:"UIL Constitution and Contest Rules 2025-26, Section 1408(e)(3)"},{Key:"ConferenceSizes",Kind:"text",Label:"High school conferences by enrolment (2024-25 and 2025-26)",Value:"6A 2275 and above; 5A 1315-2274; 4A 545-1314; 3A 254-544; 2A 105-253; 1A 104.9 and below",Source:"UIL Constitution and Contest Rules 2025-26, Section 351(b)"},{Key:"SchoolAwardLimits",Kind:"text",Label:"High school award limits",Value:"one major award up to $70 in value per student during high school enrolment at the same school, plus one additional award up to $20 per student per activity each year",Source:"UIL Constitution and Contest Rules 2025-26, Section 480(a)(1)"},{Key:"Announcements",Kind:"news",Label:"Notices printed in the rulebook",Value:[{Date:"2025-07-25",Title:"116th edition printed",Text:"The 2025-2026 Constitution and Contest Rules were printed on July 25, 2025. The UIL asks readers to see its website for the most current revisions.",Url:""},{Date:"",Title:"Academic rule changes",Text:"Appendix IV (2025-2026 rule changes) lists changes to the constitution, music and athletic rules. It lists no change to the High School or A+ academic, speech, journalism or theatre contest rules.",Url:""}],Source:"UIL Constitution and Contest Rules 2025-26, front matter and Appendix IV"}],l=[{Claim:"Rules, entry limits, qualification, points and eligibility are as printed in the 2025-26 UIL Constitution and Contest Rules (116th edition).",SourceType:"Primary - the event's own rules document",Source:"UIL Constitution and Contest Rules 2025-26",Url:"https://resources.finalsite.net/images/v1758137358/kcisdnet/qeobiutrjudepwfq0o3y/2025-2026UILConstitutionandContestRules.pdf"},{Claim:"The rulebook prints no entry fee, event dates for the year, venue or judging marks; those are set by each district, region and event handbook.",SourceType:"Primary - the event's own rules document",Source:"UIL Constitution and Contest Rules 2025-26",Url:"https://resources.finalsite.net/images/v1758137358/kcisdnet/qeobiutrjudepwfq0o3y/2025-2026UILConstitutionandContestRules.pdf"}],d={TemplateKey:e,Year:2026,Note:t,Page:n,ParticipantTypes:o,AgeGroups:i,Competitions:s,CompetitionAgeGroups:a,Variables:r,Evidence:l};export{i as AgeGroups,a as CompetitionAgeGroups,s as Competitions,l as Evidence,t as Note,n as Page,o as ParticipantTypes,e as TemplateKey,r as Variables,c as Year,d as default};
